    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-cream">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-kicker">Quick answers</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">❓ Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <details>
        <summary>How do I know if a vegetable is still safe to cook with?</summary>
        <p>If it's soft, wilted, or slightly discolored but has no mold, slime, or off smell, it's almost always fine to cook. Heat kills most surface bacteria — roasting or sautéing "tired" vegetables often makes them taste even better.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What's the single most useful ingredient for fridge cooking?</summary>
        <p>Eggs. They combine with almost any vegetable, grain, or protein and cook in under 5 minutes.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>I don't have any fresh vegetables left — now what?</summary>
        <p>Check your freezer and pantry. Frozen vegetables, canned beans, and canned tomatoes are just as valid and often get overlooked.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do I make fridge-cooking a habit, not a once-in-a-while trick?</summary>
        <p>Do a 2-minute fridge audit every few days, and lean on a tool like Fridge Chef to instantly turn what's on hand into a meal idea — less friction means more consistency.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-white">
    <div class="container wrap-narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title">🎉 Final thoughts</h2>
      <p>Next time you open the fridge and think "there's nothing here," remember: there almost always <em>is</em> something. A few eggs, some wilting greens, a nub of cheese — that's the start of dinner. 🍳🥗🍝</p>
      <p>And on the nights your brain is too tired to improvise? That's exactly what <a href="https://questmart.online/fridge-chef/">Fridge Chef</a> is there for. 🙌</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

